GLM-5.3-Flash (glm5next) CPU library, step 1 of 5: fused pooled-indexer compressor + flat recurrent-state copy.
Deployed 2026-09-19. Bit-exact. Both switches default off.

Parents (already published, byte-identical to the host files):
  engineering/2026-09-12/archive/serving/fleet-0911/parallel-unary-0911/ggml-cpu.patched.c  sha256 5bbbaf34c9994bfa925b9d369ddcad9d71d038b8ab3fba2392c4b4080fba4efd
  engineering/2026-09-12/archive/serving/fleet-0911/parallel-unary-0911/ops.patched.cpp     (labelled ops.cpp below)
Result: the CPU library deployed as glm-cpu-fast-0919, sha256 4793379e6894a9286168f79c4f323985388ec0b0ac65e54013dcd2957488af1f.

GGML_CPU_GLM_POOL_FUSION=1
  The kpool indexer re-derives every pool summary each token through a chain of graph nodes
  (get_rows -> permute/cont x2 -> add ape -> soft_max over the slot axis -> mul -> sum_rows). The fusion runs the whole
  chain at the indexer_pool_members GET_ROWS node with the original arithmetic order, and gives that node all workers.

GGML_CPU_CPY_FLAT=1
  The KDA recurrent state copy has shape [262144,1,2,1]. The stock copy splits work over ne[1], which is 1, so ONE worker
  copied 3.1 MB per layer per token while the other 14 waited. The flat path splits bytes across each device's own workers.
  (The 09-18 hypothesis that the four sockets wrote the same pages was refuted by an address probe: destinations are distinct.)

Evidence (engineering/2026-09-20/archive/sr950-strategy/GLM53-FLASH-CODEX-20260919.md):
  standalone: 21 pool cases and 14 copy cases, each twice, in five arms (production, both off, pool, copy, both), all output bytes equal.
  copy only, native greedy, outputs byte-identical: 12.65 / 14.62 / 14.17 -> 14.10 / 16.21 / 15.71 tok/s (single runs, about +11%).
  op trace: CPY was 20.4 ms of a 150 ms verify cycle on 09-18 and 1.15 ms of 117 ms after deployment.
  combined, after deployment: native 14.05 / 16.47 / 15.99 tok/s; Codex-in-Paseo 13.99 (explanation) and 15.77 (coding). Workload-specific observations.

